From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: sunxi: cpu_comm: identify the display firmware before reading it

Channel discovery pinned the A1 firmware (display.bin 4380f1b3...):
getCurCPUID at 0x8b1227b4 as the reader self-test and the pcpu_comm_dev
pointer at 0x8b22efe4. The V2 projector runs its native 16c74a28...,
the same source relinked, where code sits 0x12c lower and that global
0x210 higher. Every V2 boot failed the self-test (00a08025 00809025),
left channel_pid 0, and any kernel CALL was ACKed and never dispatched.

Keep a table of known builds and recognise the loaded one by its own
code: getCurCPUID (still the reader self-test), a lui/lw pair loading
the dev pointer, and the lui/sb pair in `elog -m sync` storing the elog
mode byte. Each pair must also form its table address. A firmware that
matches no entry is unknown and nothing is read from its data, so the
self-test still refuses what it cannot vouch for. The V2 entries were
mapped from A1 by matching the code around every reference
(tools/mips/map-fw-pins.py): 40 getCurCPUID calls and 46 dev pointer
loads, every accepted match agreeing, and the channel-table walk at
dev + 0xa8 is identical in both.

The probe-time elog readout had the opposite problem: its fixed
addresses were the V2 build's, so on the A1 it read unrelated bytes
(every bench log reads "mode=0 en2=0 wp2=0"). Take them from the
same table, report the mode by its display_cfg.xml name (0 sync,
1 async, 2 buf; the old "Mode 1 (ring buffer)" label was wrong), and
drop the commented-out writes. Nothing is written to the firmware.

The VP route IDs registered at probe start at the same addresses in
both builds and are unchanged.

--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h
@@ -593,6 +593,33 @@
 bool cpu_comm_mips_running(void);
 
 /*
+ * cpu_comm_proto.c — display firmware identity. A code site is two
+ * instruction words at a MIPS KSEG address.
+ */
+struct cc_fw_site {
+	u32 addr;
+	u32 w0, w1;
+};
+
+struct cc_fw_build {
+	const char *name;
+	u32 cpuid;			/* getCurCPUID */
+	struct cc_fw_site dev_ref;	/* loads dev_ptr */
+	u32 dev_ptr;			/* -> pcpu_comm_dev */
+	struct cc_fw_site elog_ref;	/* stores elog_mode */
+	u32 elog_mode;			/* u8: 0 sync, 1 async, 2 buf */
+	u32 elog_buf_en;		/* u8: buf-mode ring enabled */
+	u32 elog_buf_wp;		/* u32: ring write offset */
+	u32 elog_buf;			/* 2 MiB ring */
+};
+
+/* NULL when the loaded display firmware is not one we have mapped. */
+const struct cc_fw_build *cc_fw_identify(void);
+phys_addr_t cc_kseg_to_phys(u32 kseg);
+int  cc_fw_read(u32 kseg, u32 *out);
+int  cc_fw_readb(u32 kseg, u8 *out);
+
+/*
  * Read the firmware's channel table and pick a live row to address CALLs
  * to. Negative on failure, which is not fatal: the driver still loads,
  * but CALLs are ACKed by the firmware and never dispatched.
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_dev.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_dev.c
@@ -1347,39 +1347,25 @@
 	init_intrsem(0);
 
